Helm: float wavetables, attacks from the note's sample, a clearMemory hook through mopo's processor graph and a
voice kill, for WebCLAP.

- fixed_point_wave: the band-limited wavetables (11 waveforms x 64 harmonic limits x 2048 values, the second half
  the interpolation deltas) hold floats instead of doubles: 5.8 MB less memory per instance, a little faster in the
  unison loop (the lookups miss the cache less). The oscillators read them through const float pointers and still
  mix in doubles; the difference is below 1e-7 of full scale.
- Processor::clearMemory (no-op), ProcessorRouter (every processor it runs), VoiceHandler (also the global router
  and every voice): Memory::clear, and Delay, SimpleDelay (the oscillator feedback), ReverbComb, ReverbAllPass,
  Stutter and Feedback silence their buffers. Allocates nothing, so a CLAP reset can call it on the audio thread.
- Envelope: the attack outputs the value at the end of the block, as the attack's last block already does, so a
  note starts on its sample when the wrapper splits the block there (the per-sample smoothing ramps from 0 within
  the note's first block instead of a block later).
- HelmEngine::followArpeggiatorSwitch: when the arpeggiator switches, its notes end; this ran only at the start
  of process(), so a note in the block that switched it (a patch loaded, then a key) was dropped with them. It
  runs before every note on and off too.
- VoiceHandler frees a released voice once its amplitude is below -100 dB instead of mopo's 1e-16 (an
  exponential release gets there only after about seven release times: a voice of a long release kept playing,
  inaudible, for 25 s).
- HelmLfo::randomFrom, randomTo and HelmEngine::getMonoLfo: what the random waves do in the current cycle (sample
  and hold holds the second value, sample and glide goes from the first to it), for the editor to draw.
- VoiceHandler::killAllVoices and HelmEngine::killAllVoices: the arpeggiator lets go, the sustain lifts and every
  voice gets kVoiceKill (a VOICE_KILL_TIME fade); process until no voice is active.


